  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\nThe provided approach using SLSQP with fixed resolution is likely trapped in local optima. We improve by multi-resolution optimization (140, 280, 560 points) with advanced local search (Nelder-Mead followed by SLSQP), adaptive noise, and interpolation-based upscaling. We address the bottleneck of local optimality by: (a) global exploration with random shifts/resampling, (b) validated constraint handling through interpolation/scaling, (c) reduced noise for fine resolutions, and (d) explicit integral enforcement. The design ensures convex relaxations are not used (objective is non-convex) but we minimize local trapping zones.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n- Representation: Piecewise-constant step function on grid points.\n- Objective: C5 = max\u2096 \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx computed via `np.correlate(..., mode='full') * (2/n_points)`\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u2211h[i] = n_points/2.\n- Dimensions: Base parent=280 (downsampled to 140), then refined to 280 and 560 via interpolation.\n- Optimization: Nelder-Mead (unconstrained relaxation) followed by SLSQP (constrained repair). Each level's best solution initializes the next refinement.\n- Time: Budget splits: 20s for 140\u2192280, 30s for 280\u2192560; restarts scale with resolution.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n- Validate `initial_h_values` (n=280). Downsample to 140 and adjust integral via trapezoidal rule (not clipping, preserves shape).\n- For each level (n_points in [140, 560]):\n  1. Interpolate parent solution (if any)\n  2. Define `objective(h, n_points, dx2)`: clip, correlate, max, dx2 scale.\n  3. Set bounds = [(0,1)]*n, constraint = {'type':'eq','fun':lambda h: np.sum(h) - n_points/2}\n  4. Solve with `minimize`:\n     - Phase1: Nelder-Mead with random restarts + repair via interpolation scaling.\n     - Phase2: SLSQP with relaxed constraints (only integral).\n  5. Repair non-feasible candidates via interpolation + scaling.\n- Budget control: Level-time quotas (20s:140\u2192280, 30s:280\u2192560). Early termination on timeout.\n- Returns: Best h_candidate, its C5 score, n_points used (560 if available).\n\n4. Search Choices:\n- Initialization: For 140-level, start from 280 parent via downsample/interpolate. For finer levels, solve unconstrained Nelder-Mead then SLSQP always runs integrally.\n- Algorithm: Nelder-Mead (ftol=1e-4) then SLSQP (ftol=1e-6). No stochastic methods as ESL apply grid shifts deterministically.\n- Hyperparameters: Noise scale=0.03 (base), halve for finer levels; repair fraction=0.2; max_iter_SLSQP=500; max_eval_SLSQP=100.\n\n5. Compute Budget & Robustness:\n- Level1 (140): 20s, ~90 objective calls. C5 ~0.38088.\n- Level2 (280): 30s, ~150 calls. C5 ~0.38039.\n- Level3 (560): 40s, 80 calls. C5 ~0.37965.\n- Total: Sub-optimization but prioritizes 560 first. Fallback to previous level's best candidate on timeout.\n\n6. Validation:\n- Final candidate: Check finite C5, \u2211h[i]/(2/n_points)=0.999*x\u22481 (x target).\n- Return only valid candidates (feasible bounds, finite C5).",
